  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nA weak mid-level shortwave trough may drop across the area this\nevening, in an environment where even the \"high-end\" CAPE will\nbe a couple hundred J/kg. Given the weak forcing and low\ninstability, a few showers and storm day develop, though severe\nthreats are not anticipated. The same can generally be said for\nSaturday afternoon, though CAPE may be a bit higher (up to 1000\nJ/kg), though dry air aloft and a subsidence inversion favors\nkeeping convection shallow. SPC and ML outlooks to not favor\nsevere development.\n\nSunday, only a low chance for severe weather exists. Mid level\nflow and organized storms favors tracks over the midwest to \nwest-central Ohio. The potential of storms only exists on some \nlake boundary or outflow, but even then sfc-6km flow is quite \nslow (>20kts), and DCAPE decreases as moisture returns with LREF\n50th percentile DCAPE ~600 J/kg, assuming a parcel maximizes \nits potential. If anything, this would allude to a marginal \nflood threat. That being said, if substantial upper level wind \nforecasts change, resulting in increasing MCS potential to the \neast of the current forecast, our chances would go up locally.\n\nThe potential for widespread strong to severe storms becomes \nmore favorable Monday into Tuesday as the ridge over the \nnorthern Great Lakes weakens, opening the door for upstream MCSs\nto propagate into the region or that remnant outflow boundaries\nof decaying MCSs redevelopment during peak heating. If\nmesoscale convective systems (MCSs) do develop and track into \nour region, forecast soundings indicate an environment capable \nof supporting all severe weather hazards, including hail, \ndamaging wind gusts, tornadoes, and heavy rainfall. And most\nimportantly, there is low confidence in when if if these should\noccur locally.\n\nNCAR Machine-learning guidance has a 5-10% of severe weather\nover our region on Sunday; 15 to 45% chance for Monday and\nTuesday. Colorado State University (CSU) machine learning has \n5-15% chance of excessive rainfall (flash flooding) Monday into \nTuesday. The Weather Prediction Center has issued \"Marginal\" (1 \nout of 4) risks for back-to-back days. \n\nThe eventual evolution of this threat will largely depend on \nthe amplitude of the eastern U.S. trough and the strength and \nposition of the upper- level ridge, which will influence the \ntrack and intensity of any MCSs.\n\nThe threat for severe storms and precipitation is expected to\nremain low Wednesday into Thursday in the wake of a departing\ncold front and building high pressure.",
